"First, we solve the equation $x(x-3)=1$. This can be rewritten as $x^2 - 3x - 1 = 0$.\n\nNext, we use the quadratic formula $x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}$, where $a = 1$, $b = -3$, and $c = -1$.\n\nPlugging in the values, we get:\n$$x = \\frac{-(-3) \\pm \\sqrt{(-3)^2 - 4(1)(-1)}}{2(1)} = \\frac{3 \\pm \\sqrt{9 + 4}}{2} = \\frac{3 \\pm \\sqrt{13}}{2}.$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $\\frac{3 + \\sqrt{13}}{2}$ and $\\frac{3 - \\sqrt{13}}{2}$.\n\nNow, we identify $a = 3$, $b = 13$, and $c = 2$.\n\nFinally, we calculate $abc$ as:\n$$abc = 3 \\cdot 13 \\cdot 2 = \\boxed{78}.$$"
"Let the solutions of the quadratic equation \\(2x^2 + 13x + 6 = 0\\) be \\(p\\) and \\(q\\). By Vieta's formulas, we know:\n\n\\[\np + q = -\\frac{13}{2} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad pq = \\frac{6}{2}\n\\]\n\nWe need to find the product of the squares of the solutions, which is \\((p^2)(q^2)\\). Using the identity \\((p^2)(q^2) = (p + q)^2 - 2pq\\), we have:\n\n\\[\n(p^2)(q^2) = \\left(-\\frac{13}{2}\\right)^2 - 2 \\left(\\frac{6}{2}\\right)\n\\]\n\nCalculating each term:\n\n\\[\n(p^2)(q^2) = \\frac{169}{4} - 6 = \\frac{169 - 24}{4} = \\frac{145}{4}\n\\]\n\nThus, the product of the squares of the solutions is \\(\\boxed{37}\\)."
