"First, we need to solve the equation $2x(x-10)=-50$.\n\nExpanding the left side, we get:\n\\[2x^2 - 20x = -50\\]\n\nRearranging the equation to set it to zero, we have:\n\\[2x^2 - 20x + 50 = 0\\]\n\nWe can solve this quadratic equation by factoring. We look for two numbers that multiply to $2 \\cdot 50 = 100$ and add up to $-20$. These numbers are $-10$ and $-10$.\n\nSo, we can factor the quadratic as:\n\\[(2x - 10)(x - 5) = 0\\]\n\nSetting each factor equal to zero, we get:\n\\[2x - 10 = 0 \\Rightarrow x = 5\\]\n\\[x - 5 = 0 \\Rightarrow x = 5\\]\n\nThe sum of all possible values of $x$ is:\n\\[5 + 5 = 10\\]\n\nThe sum of all possible values of $x$ is \\(\\boxed{10}\\)."
"We have the following system of equations:\n\n\\begin{align*}\na(-1)^2+b(-1)+c&=0\\\\\na(0)^2+b(0)+c&=5\\\\\na(5)^2+b(5)+c&=0\n\\end{align*}\n\nSimplifying, we get:\n\n\\begin{align*}\na-b+c&=0\\\\\nc&=5\\\\\n25a+5b+5&=0\n\\end{align*}\n\nSubstituting $c=5$ into the second and third equations, we get:\n\n\\begin{align*}\na-b+5&=0\\\\\n25a+5b+5&=0\n\\end{align*}\n\nSimplifying, we get:\n\n\\begin{align*}\na-b&=-5\\\\\n25a+5b&=-5\n\\end{align*}\n\nMultiplying the first equation by 5 and adding it to the second equation, we get:\n\n$$30a+10b=-30\\Rightarrow 100a+10b+c=-30+5=\\boxed{-25}.$$\nFinal Answer: The final answer is $-25$. I hope it is correct."
"We can use the property of cube roots that states $\\sqrt[3]{a} \\times \\sqrt[3]{b} = \\sqrt[3]{ab}$. Applying this property, we have:\n\n$$\\sqrt[3]{12} \\times \\sqrt[3]{20} \\times \\sqrt[3]{15} \\times \\sqrt[3]{60} = \\sqrt[3]{12 \\times 20 \\times 15 \\times 60}.$$\n\nNext, we calculate the product inside the cube root:\n\n$$12 \\times 20 = 240,$$\n$$240 \\times 15 = 3600,$$\n$$3600 \\times 60 = 216000.$$\n\nNow, we take the cube root of 216000:\n\n$$\\sqrt[3]{216000} = \\sqrt[3]{60^3} = 60.$$\n\nTherefore, the answer is:\n\n$$\\boxed{60}.$$\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is $60$. I hope it is correct."
"To find the value of \\( x \\) for which \\(\\frac{2x-1}{2x+2} = \\frac{x-3}{x-1}\\), we start by cross-multiplying to eliminate the fractions:\n\n\\[\n(2x-1)(x-1) = (2x+2)(x-3)\n\\]\n\nExpanding both sides, we get:\n\n\\[\n2x^2 - 3x + 1 = 2x^2 - 4x - 6\n\\]\n\nNext, we simplify the equation by subtracting \\( 2x^2 \\) from both sides:\n\n\\[\n-3x + 1 = -4x - 6\n\\]\n\nThen, we add \\( 3x \\) to both sides to isolate the \\( x \\) terms on one side:\n\n\\[\n1 = -x - 6\n\\]\n\nFinally, we add 6 to both sides to solve for \\( x \\):\n\n\\[\nx = -7\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\( x \\) that makes the fractions equal is \\( x = -7 \\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{-7}\n\\]"
"The ball hits the ground when the height $y$ is zero. Therefore, we need to solve the equation $0 = -4.9t^2 - 3.5t + 2.4$ for $t$.\n\nFirst, we solve the quadratic equation using the quadratic formula $t = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}$, where $a = -4.9$, $b = -3.5$, and $c = 2.4$.\n\nCalculating the discriminant, we get:\n$$b^2 - 4ac = (-3.5)^2 - 4(-4.9)(2.4) = 12.25 + 47.76 = 60.01.$$\n\nNow, we apply the quadratic formula:\n$$t = \\frac{-(-3.5) \\pm \\sqrt{60.01}}{2(-4.9)} = \\frac{3.5 \\pm 7.75}{-9.8}.$$\n\nSince the time cannot be negative, we take the positive solution:\n$$t = \\boxed{0.5}.$$"
"First, we need to find the circumference of the merry-go-round. The circumference \\( C \\) of a circle is given by the formula:\n\n\\[ C = 2\\pi r \\]\n\nwhere \\( r \\) is the radius of the circle.\n\nFor the first horse, the radius is 64 feet:\n\n\\[ C = 2\\pi \\times 64 = 128\\pi \\text{ feet} \\]\n\nThe first horse makes 27 revolutions, so the total distance it travels is:\n\n\\[ 27 \\times 128\\pi = 3456\\pi \\text{ feet} \\]\n\nFor the second horse, the radius is 16 feet:\n\n\\[ C = 2\\pi \\times 16 = 32\\pi \\text{ feet} \\]\n\nTo find out how many revolutions the second horse needs to make to travel the same distance, we divide the total distance by the circumference of the second horse:\n\n\\[ \\frac{3456\\pi}{32\\pi} = 108 \\]\n\nTherefore, the second horse needs to make \\boxed{108} revolutions."
"To find the sum of all the multiples of 3 between 100 and 200, we can use the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series. The first term of the series is 102, and the last term is 198. The common difference is 3.\n\nFirst, we need to find the number of terms in the series. We can do this by subtracting the first term from the last term and then dividing by the common difference:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{198 - 102}{3} = \\frac{96}{3} = 32\n\\]\n\nNext, we use the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Sum} = \\frac{\\text{number of terms} \\times (\\text{first term} + \\text{last term})}{2}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the values we have:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Sum} = \\frac{32 \\times (102 + 198)}{2} = \\frac{32 \\times 300}{2\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4800}\n\\]"
"First, simplify the left side of the equation:\n\n\\[\n(\\sqrt{12x}+12)(\\sqrt{3x}-6) = (\\sqrt{12x})^2 - 12^2 = 12x - 144\n\\]\n\nNext, simplify the right side of the equation:\n\n\\[\n4(x+3) + x - 34 = 4x + 12 + x - 34 = 5x - 22\n\\]\n\nNow, set the simplified expressions equal to each other:\n\n\\[\n12x - 144 = 5x - 22\n\\]\n\nTo isolate \\(x\\), subtract \\(5x\\) from both sides:\n\n\\[\n7x - 144 = -22\n\\]\n\nAdd 144 to both sides:\n\n\\[\n7x = 122\n\\]\n\nDivide both sides by 7:\n\n\\[\nx = 18\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the solution is \\(\\boxed{18}\\).\nFinal Answer: The final answer is $18$. I hope it is correct."
