"To determine for how many positive integers \\( n \\) the expression \\( n^2 - 3n + 2 \\) is a prime number, we first need to analyze the quadratic expression.\n\nThe quadratic expression \\( n^2 - 3n + 2 \\) can be factored as follows:\n\\[\nn^2 - 3n + 2 = (n-1)(n-2)\n\\]\n\nFor \\( n^2 - 3n + 2 \\) to be a prime number, both factors \\( (n-1) \\) and \\( (n-2) \\) must be 1, because a prime number has only two positive divisors: 1 and itself.\n\nThus, we need:\n\\[\nn-1 = 1 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad n-2 = 1\n\\]\n\nSolving these equations:\n\\[\nn = 2 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad n = 3\n\\]\n\nTherefore, there are exactly two positive integers \\( n \\) for which \\( n^2 - 3n + 2 \\) is a prime number.\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{2}\n\\]"
