"To find the first term of the infinite geometric series, we can use the formula for the sum of an infinite geometric series:\n\n\\[ S = \\frac{a}{1 - r} \\]\n\nwhere \\( S \\) is the sum of the series, \\( a \\) is the first term, and \\( r \\) is the common ratio.\n\nGiven:\n- \\( S = 45 \\)\n- \\( r = -\\frac{1}{2} \\)\n\nWe need to solve for \\( a \\):\n\n\\[ 45 = \\frac{a}{1 - \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)} \\]\n\nSimplify the denominator:\n\n\\[ 45 = \\frac{a}{1 + \\frac{1}{2}} \\]\n\\[ 45 = \\frac{a}{\\frac{3}{2}} \\]\n\nTo solve for \\( a \\), multiply both sides by \\(\\frac{3}{2}\\):\n\n\\[ a = 45 \\times \\frac{3}{2} \\]\n\\[ a = 67.5 \\]\n\nTherefore, the first term of the series is\n\n\\[ \\boxed{67.5} \\]"
