"For a quadratic equation \\(ax^2 + bx + c = 0\\) to have only one solution, its discriminant must be zero. The discriminant \\(\\Delta\\) of a quadratic equation \\(ax^2 + bx + c = 0\\) is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta = b^2 - 4ac\n\\]\n\nFor the given equation \\(ax^2 + 8x + 4 = 0\\), we have \\(a = a\\), \\(b = 8\\), and \\(c = 4\\). Substituting these values into the discriminant formula, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta = 8^2 - 4a \\cdot 4 = 64 - 16a\n\\]\n\nFor the equation to have only one solution, the discriminant must be zero:\n\n\\[\n64 - 16a = 0\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(a\\), we get:\n\n\\[\n64 = 16a\n\\]\n\n\\[\na = \\frac{64}{16} = 4\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\(a\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4}\n\\]"
