"Given the quadratic equation \\(x^2 - 2x - 3 = 0\\), we know that the roots \\(p\\) and \\(q\\) satisfy the relationships:\n\\[\np + q = 2 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad pq = -3.\n\\]\n\nWe need to find the value of \\((p + 1)(q + 1)\\). Expanding this expression, we get:\n\\[\n(p + 1)(q + 1) = pq + (p + q) + 1.\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the known values from the quadratic equation:\n\\[\n(p + 1)(q + 1) = -3 + 2 + 1.\n\\]\n\nSimplifying this, we find:\n\\[\n(p + 1)(q + 1) = 0.\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\((p + 1)(q + 1)\\) is \\(\\boxed{0}\\).\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is $0$. I hope it is correct."
"First, we need to determine the slope of the line. The slope \\(m\\) of a line passing through two points \\((x_1, y_1)\\) and \\((x_2, y_2)\\) is given by:\n\n\\[ m = \\frac{y_2 - y_1}{x_2 - x_1} \\]\n\nFrom the graph, we can see that the line passes through the points \\((-10, -2.71)\\) and \\((10, 8.71)\\). Plugging these coordinates into the slope formula:\n\n\\[ m = \\frac{8.71 - (-2.71)}{10 - (-10)} = \\frac{11.42}{20} = 0.571 \\]\n\nNow, we use this slope to find the \\(y\\)-coordinate of the point \\((1001, n)\\):\n\n\\[ n = m \\cdot 1001 = 0.571 \\cdot 1001 = 571 \\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\(n\\) is \\(571\\)."
