"To find the shortest distance between the two circles, we need to determine the distance between their centers and then subtract the sum of their radii from this distance.\n\nThe center of the first circle is $(9, 5)$ and its radius is $\\sqrt{6.25} = 2.5$.\nThe center of the second circle is $(-6, -3)$ and its radius is $\\sqrt{49} = 7$.\n\nThe distance between the centers of the two circles is given by the distance formula:\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{(9 - (-6))^2 + (5 - (-3))^2} = \\sqrt{15^2 + 8^2} = \\sqrt{225 + 64} = \\sqrt{289} = 17\n\\]\n\nThe sum of the radii of the two circles is:\n\n\\[\n2.5 + 7 = 9.5\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the shortest distance between the two circles is:\n\n\\[\n17 - 9.5 = 7.5\n\\]\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is \\(7.5\\). I hope it is correct."
