"To determine the degree of the polynomial \\(2f(x) + 4g(x)\\), we need to consider the degrees of the individual polynomials \\(f(x)\\) and \\(g(x)\\).\n\nGiven:\n- \\(f(x)\\) is a polynomial of degree 3.\n- \\(g(x)\\) is a polynomial of degree 5.\n\nWhen we add or subtract polynomials, the degree of the resulting polynomial is determined by the term with the highest degree. Therefore, we need to consider the degrees of the terms \\(2f(x)\\) and \\(4g(x)\\).\n\n- The degree of \\(2f(x)\\) is \\(2 \\times 3 = 6\\).\n- The degree of \\(4g(x)\\) is \\(4 \\times 5 = 20\\).\n\nThe degree of the polynomial \\(2f(x) + 4g(x)\\) is the highest degree among the two terms. In this case, the term \\(4g(x)\\) has the highest degree, which is 20.\n\nThus, the degree of the polynomial \\(2f(x) + 4g(x)\\) is:\n\n\\(\\boxed{20}\\)"
