"To determine the number of polynomials of the form \\( x^2 + mx + n \\) with positive integer roots and \\( n = 28 \\), we need to find all pairs of positive integers \\((p, q)\\) such that their product \\( pq = 28 \\).\n\nFirst, we list all the positive integer pairs of 28:\n\\[ (1, 28), (2, 14), (4, 7), (7, 4), (14, 2), (28, 1) \\]\n\nEach pair \\((p, q)\\) represents a root of the polynomial \\( x^2 + mx + n \\). Since the roots of the polynomial are symmetric, each pair \\((p, q)\\) and \\((q, p)\\) will yield the same polynomial. Therefore, we only need to count the unique pairs.\n\nFrom the list above, we see that there are 6 unique pairs.\n\nThus, the number of polynomials of the form \\( x^2 + mx + n \\) with positive integer roots and \\( n = 28 \\) is 6.\n\nThe final answer is \\(\\boxed{6}\\)."
"To find the domain of the function \\( f(x) = \\frac{x+2}{x^2-2x-24} \\), we need to determine the values of \\( x \\) for which the function is defined. The function is undefined where the denominator is zero.\n\nFirst, we solve the equation \\( x^2 - 2x - 24 = 0 \\):\n\n\\[\nx^2 - 2x - 24 = (x - 6)(x + 4) = 0\n\\]\n\nThis gives us the solutions \\( x = 6 \\) and \\( x = -4 \\). Therefore, the function is undefined at \\( x = 6 \\) and \\( x = -4 \\).\n\nThe domain of the function is all real numbers except \\( x = 6 \\) and \\( x = -4 \\). In interval notation, this is:\n\n\\[\n(-\\infty, -4) \\cup (-4, 6) \\cup (6, \\infty)\n\\]\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is \\( \\boxed{(-\\infty, -4) \\cup (-4, 6) \\cup (6, \\infty)} \\)."
"To simplify \\((2+3i)(1-2i)\\), we use the distributive property (also known as the FOIL method for binomials):\n\n\\begin{align*}\n(2+3i)(1-2i) &= 2 \\cdot 1 + 2 \\cdot (-2i) + 3i \\cdot 1 + 3i \\cdot (-2i) \\\\\n&= 2 - 4i + 3i - 6i^2.\n\\end{align*}\n\nNext, we recall that \\(i^2 = -1\\). Therefore, we substitute \\(-1\\) for \\(i^2\\):\n\n\\begin{align*}\n2 - 4i + 3i - 6(-1) &= 2 - 4i + 3i + 6 \\\\\n&= 2 + 6 + (-4i + 3i) \\\\\n&= 8 - i.\n\\end{align*}\n\nThus, the simplified form of \\((2+3i)(1-2i)\\) is \\(\\boxed{8-i}\\).\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is \\(8 - i\\). I hope it is correct."
"To find the middle term of the arithmetic sequence, we first need to determine the sums of the numbers at the ends of each line segment. Let's denote the numbers as follows:\n- \\(A = 3\\)\n- \\(B = 5\\)\n- \\(C = 6\\)\n- \\(D = 7\\)\n- \\(E = 9\\)\n\nThe sums of the numbers at the ends of the line segments are:\n- \\(\\overline{AB} = 3 + 5 = 8\\)\n- \\(\\overline{BC} = 5 + 6 = 11\\)\n- \\(\\overline{CD} = 6 + 7 = 13\\)\n- \\(\\overline{DE} = 7 + 9 = 16\\)\n- \\(\\overline{EA} = 9 + 3 = 12\\)\n\nThese sums form an arithmetic sequence: 8, 11, 13, 16, 12.\n\nThe middle term of an arithmetic sequence is the third term. Therefore, the middle term of this sequence is \\(13\\).\n\nThe middle term of the arithmetic sequence is \\(\\boxed{13}\\)."
