"To rewrite the quadratic \\(x^2 + bx + 1\\) in the form \\((x + m)^2 - 63\\), we need to complete the square.\n\nStarting with \\(x^2 + bx + 1\\):\n\n1. Take the coefficient of \\(x\\), which is \\(b\\), and divide it by 2 to get \\(\\frac{b}{2}\\).\n2. Square \\(\\frac{b}{2}\\) to get \\(\\frac{b^2}{4}\\).\n3. Add and subtract \\(\\frac{b^2}{4}\\) inside the expression.\n\nSo, we have:\n\\[x^2 + bx + \\frac{b^2}{4} - \\frac{b^2}{4} + 1 = (x + \\frac{b}{2})^2 - \\frac{b^2}{4} + 1.\\]\n\nWe want this to equal \\((x + m)^2 - 63\\). Therefore, we need:\n\\[\\frac{b^2}{4} = 63 \\Rightarrow b^2 = 252 \\cdot 4 = 256 \\Rightarrow b = \\boxed{16}.\\]"
