"To solve the equation \\(\\frac{2}{3} = \\frac{4}{x-5}\\), we can cross-multiply to eliminate the fractions:\n\n\\[\n2(x-5) = 3 \\cdot 4\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n2x - 10 = 12\n\\]\n\nNext, we solve for \\(x\\) by isolating the variable. First, add 10 to both sides:\n\n\\[\n2x - 10 + 10 = 12 + 10\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n2x = 22\n\\]\n\nNow, divide both sides by 2:\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{22}{2}\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[\nx = 11\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the solution to the equation is \\(\\boxed{11}\\)."
"To find the numerical value of \\( x = \\frac{1}{2 - \\frac{1}{2 - \\frac{1}{2 - \\ldots}}} \\), we start by letting \\( x \\) be the value of the entire expression. This gives us the equation:\n\n\\[ x = \\frac{1}{2 - \\frac{1}{2 - \\frac{1}{2 - \\ldots}}} \\]\n\nSince the expression inside the fraction is the same as the entire expression, we can substitute \\( x \\) into the equation:\n\n\\[ x = \\frac{1}{2 - x} \\]\n\nNext, we solve this equation for \\( x \\). First, we eliminate the fraction by multiplying both sides by \\( 2 - x \\):\n\n\\[ x(2 - x) = 1 \\]\n\\[ 2x - x^2 = 1 \\]\n\\[ 2x - x^2 = 1 \\]\n\\[ 2x - x^2 = 1 \\]\n\\[ x = 1 \\]\n\nThus, the numerical value of \\( x \\) is \\(\\boxed{1}\\).\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is 1."
"To determine how much money Frederick will have when he can collect the money at the age of 18, we need to calculate the simple interest earned over 18 years at a rate of 5% per year and then add it to the principal amount.\n\nThe formula for simple interest is:\n\\[ \\text{Simple Interest} = \\text{Principal} \\times \\text{Rate} \\times \\text{Time} \\]\n\nHere, the principal is \\$2000, the rate is 5% per year, and the time is 18 years.\n\nFirst, calculate the simple interest:\n\\[ \\text{Simple Interest} = 2000 \\times 0.05 \\times 18 = 2000 \\times 0.9 = 1800 \\]\n\nNext, add the simple interest to the principal amount:\n\\[ \\text{Total Amount} = 2000 + 1800 = 3800 \\]\n\nTherefore, the amount of money Frederick will have when he can collect the money at the age of 18 is \\(\\boxed{3800}\\).}"
