"First, we calculate the total number of ways to choose 3 cards from a 52-card deck. This is given by the combination formula $\\binom{52}{3} = \\frac{52 \\cdot 51 \\cdot 50}{3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1} = 22100$.\n\nNext, we calculate the number of ways to choose 3 cards of the same color. There are 4 suits, and each suit has 13 cards. The number of ways to choose 3 cards from one suit is $\\binom{13}{3} = 286$. Therefore, the number of ways to choose 3 cards of the same color is $4 \\cdot 286 = 1144$.\n\nThe number of ways to choose 3 cards of different colors is $22100 - 1144 = 20956$.\n\nThus, the probability that the three cards chosen are not all the same color is $\\frac{20956}{22100} = \\boxed{\\frac{95}{100}}$.\n\nI hope it is correct."
