"To determine the number of ways Mary can choose three tourist locations out of five, we use the combination formula:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{n}{k} = \\frac{n!}{k!(n-k)!}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\( n \\) is the total number of items to choose from, and \\( k \\) is the number of items to choose.\n\nHere, \\( n = 5 \\) and \\( k = 3 \\):\n\n\\[\n\\binom{5}{3} = \\frac{5!}{3!(5-3)!} = \\frac{5!}{3! \\cdot 2!}\n\\]\n\nCalculating the factorials:\n\n\\[\n5! = 5 \\times 4 \\times 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 120\n\\]\n\\[\n3! = 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 6\n\\]\n\\[\n2! = 2 \\times 1 = 2\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\binom{5}{3} = \\frac{120}{6 \\times 2} = \\frac{120}{12} = \\boxed{10}"
"To determine the probability of spinning an odd number on the given spinner, we first identify the numbers on the spinner. The numbers are 15, 23, and 14.\n\nNext, we identify which of these numbers are odd. An odd number is any integer that is not divisible by 2.\n\n- 15 is odd because it is not divisible by 2.\n- 23 is odd because it is not divisible by 2.\n- 14 is even because it is divisible by 2.\n\nThus, the odd numbers on the spinner are 15 and 23. There are 2 odd numbers out of a total of 3 numbers on the spinner.\n\nThe probability of an event is given by the ratio of the number of favorable outcomes to the total number of possible outcomes. Therefore, the probability of spinning an odd number is:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Probability} = \\frac{\\text{Number of odd numbers}}{\\text{Total number of numbers}} = \\frac{2}{3}\n\\]\n\nThus, the probability of spinning an odd number is expressed as:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{2}{3}}\n\\]"
