"To determine the number of distinct arrangements of the word \"PAPA,\" we need to consider the composition of the word. The word \"PAPA\" consists of 4 letters, where the letter \"P\" appears twice.\n\nFirst, we calculate the total number of permutations of 4 letters if all letters were distinct. This is given by the factorial of 4:\n\n\\[\n4! = 4 \\times 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 24\n\\]\n\nHowever, since the letter \"P\" appears twice, we need to account for the repetition. We do this by dividing by the factorial of the number of times \"P\" appears, which is \\(2!\\):\n\n\\[\n2! = 2 \\times 1 = 2\n\\]\n\nThus, the number of distinct arrangements of the word \"PAPA\" is:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{4!}{2!} = \\frac{24}{2} = 12\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the number of distinct arrangements of the word \"PAPA\" is \\(\\boxed{12}\\).\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is \\(12\\). I hope it is correct."
"To find the remainder when \\(301^4\\) is divided by 10,000, we can use modular arithmetic. First, we simplify \\(301 \\mod 1000\\):\n\n\\[301 \\mod 1000 = 301 - 300 \\cdot 1 = 301 - 300 = 101\\]\n\nNow, we need to find \\(101^4 \\mod 10000\\):\n\n\\[101^4 = 1040401\\]\n\nNext, we find \\(1040401 \\mod 10000\\):\n\n\\[1040401 \\mod 10000 = 1040401 - 10000 \\cdot 104 = 1040401 - 1040000 = 0401\\]\n\nTherefore, the remainder when \\(301^4\\) is divided by 10,000 is \\(\\boxed{0401}\\)."
"To determine the number of different pizzas Sarah can order, we need to calculate the number of ways to choose 2 toppings out of 9. This is a combination problem, where the order of selection does not matter.\n\nThe formula for combinations is given by:\n\\[ \\binom{n}{r} = \\frac{n!}{r!(n-r)!} \\]\n\nIn this case, \\( n = 9 \\) (the total number of toppings) and \\( r = 2 \\) (the number of toppings Sarah wants to choose).\n\nSo, we have:\n\\[ \\binom{9}{2} = \\frac{9!}{2!(9-2)!} = \\frac{9!}{2! \\cdot 7!} \\]\n\nWe can simplify this by canceling out the \\( 7! \\) in the numerator and the denominator:\n\\[ \\binom{9}{2} = \\frac{9 \\times 8 \\times 7!}{2 \\times 1 \\times 7!} = \\frac{9 \\times 8}{2} = 36 \\]\n\nTherefore, the number of different pizzas Sarah can order is \\(\\boxed{36} \\)."
"To find the probability that the team will win its next game, we need to determine the ratio of wins to total games played. The team averages 7 losses for every 13 wins, which means the total number of games played is \\(7 + 13 = 20\\).\n\nThe probability of winning a game is the number of wins divided by the total number of games played:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Probability of winning} = \\frac{\\text{Number of wins}}{\\text{Total number of games}} = \\frac{13}{20}\n\\]\n\nTo express this probability as a percentage, we multiply by 100:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Probability of winning} = \\left( \\frac{13}{20} \\right) \\times 100\n\\]\n\nCalculating this gives:\n\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{13}{20} \\right) \\times 100 = 65\\%\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the probability that the team will win its next game is \\(\\boxed{65\\%}\\).\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is $65\\%$. I hope it is correct."
