"The binomial coefficient $\\dbinom{12}{9}$ is calculated using the formula:\n\n\\[\n\\dbinom{12}{9} = \\frac{12!}{9!(12-9)!} = \\frac{12!}{9! \\cdot 3!}\n\\]\n\nWe can simplify this by canceling out the common terms in the numerator and the denominator:\n\n\\[\n\\dbinom{12}{9} = \\frac{12 \\times 11 \\times 10 \\times 9!}{9! \\times 3!} = \\frac{12 \\times 11 \\times 10}{3!}\n\\]\n\nNext, we calculate $3!$:\n\n\\[\n3! = 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 6\n\\]\n\nSo,\n\n\\[\n\\dbinom{12}{9} = \\frac{12 \\times 11 \\times 10}{6} = \\frac{1320}{6} = 220\n\\]\n\nTherefore,\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{220}\n\\]"
"To determine the number of ways to choose three starters from the four students (Lance, Sally, Joy, and Fred), we can use the concept of combinations. The order in which the students are chosen does not matter, so we use the combination formula:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{n}{r} = \\frac{n!}{r!(n-r)!}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\( n \\) is the total number of items to choose from, and \\( r \\) is the number of items to choose.\n\nHere, \\( n = 4 \\) and \\( r = 3 \\):\n\n\\[\n\\binom{4}{3} = \\frac{4!}{3!(4-3)!} = \\frac{4!}{3! \\cdot 1!}\n\\]\n\nSince \\( 1! = 1 \\), we have:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{4}{3} = \\frac{4!}{3! \\cdot 1} = \\frac{4!}{3!} = 4\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the number of ways to choose three starters is \\(\\boxed{4}\\).\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is $4$. I hope it is correct."
"The binomial coefficient $\\dbinom{22}{19}$ is defined as $\\frac{22!}{19! \\cdot 3!}$. We can simplify this by canceling out the common terms in the numerator and the denominator:\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\dbinom{22}{19} &= \\frac{22!}{19! \\cdot 3!} \\\\\n&= \\frac{22 \\cdot 21 \\cdot 20 \\cdot 19!}{19! \\cdot 3!} \\\\\n&= \\frac{22 \\cdot 21 \\cdot 20}{3!} \\\\\n&= \\frac{22 \\cdot 21 \\cdot 20}{3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{22 \\cdot 21 \\cdot 20}{6} \\\\\n&= \\frac{9240}{6} \\\\\n&= \\boxed{1540}.\n\\end{align*}\n\nTherefore, $\\dbinom{22}{19} = \\boxed{1540}$.\nFinal Answer: The final answer is $1540$."
