"To determine the integer factor by which \\(9!\\) must be multiplied to equal \\(11!\\), we start by expressing \\(11!\\) in terms of \\(9!\\).\n\nThe factorial of a number \\(n\\), denoted as \\(n!\\), is the product of all positive integers from 1 to \\(n\\). Therefore, \\(11!\\) can be written as:\n\\[\n11! = 11 \\times 10 \\times 9!\n\\]\n\nWe need to find the factor by which \\(9!\\) must be multiplied to get \\(11!\\). This factor is given by the ratio of \\(11!\\) to \\(9!\\):\n\\[\n\\frac{11!}{9!} = \\frac{11 \\times 10 \\times 9!}{9!}\n\\]\n\nThe \\(9!\\) terms cancel each other out:\n\\[\n\\frac{11!}{9!} = 11 \\times 10\n\\]\n\nThus, the integer factor by which \\(9!\\) must be multiplied to equal \\(11!\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{110 \\times 10}\n\\]"
